00:17:40.325 --> 00:17:41.205
make

00:17:41.205 --> 00:17:42.645
a differential assessment

00:17:43.205 --> 00:17:46.165
if there was one of these two stream corridors.

00:17:47.285 --> 00:17:49.045
And at that time, as you remember,

00:17:49.445 --> 00:17:50.005
there had been

00:17:51.605 --> 00:17:56.500
the term riparian corridor or riparian habitat had been put forth.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.380
And so that's, as you saw from my report,

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:01.300
went into

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:05.700
talking about what that is and basically admitted that technically

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:06.900
any

00:18:07.395 --> 00:18:12.195
corridor that is associated with a stream, whether the stream is intermittent or perennial,

00:18:12.515 --> 00:18:17.155
is a riparian corridor. There's no doubt to that. But amid the differentiation that

00:18:17.875 --> 00:18:18.595
in

00:18:18.995 --> 00:18:23.610
the real world, so we speak, there is a gamut of functionality

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:24.250
between

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:29.770
intermittent streams with a small watershed versus ones with a bigger watershed versus

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:32.090
what we would call semi perennial streams.

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:36.250
Usually those are the ones for a variety of reasons might have

00:18:36.965 --> 00:18:39.845
more water flowing for a longer period of time before they

00:18:40.085 --> 00:18:43.685
dry out in the summer. And of course, perennial streams.

00:18:43.845 --> 00:18:47.365
So there are services, ecological services that

00:18:47.605 --> 00:18:49.445
are much more pronounced

00:18:49.445 --> 00:18:56.090
in perennial streams and then lesser and lesser as you go into intermittent streams. Of course, every stream has

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:57.690
to be looked at as site specifically.

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:04.250
So I did that and I walked up the smaller stream, which by the way, immediately

00:19:05.685 --> 00:19:06.565
from that

00:19:06.885 --> 00:19:09.765
little Panhandle that comes off the road,

00:19:10.325 --> 00:19:13.605
which is the right of way, I guess, for a potential

00:19:14.165 --> 00:19:15.205
driveway, you

00:19:16.005 --> 00:19:18.645
can see that the Western Stream

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:25.590
climbs up a little bit up in the topography, but for the most part, it's a wider corridor.

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:29.990
It's it's flatter or more gentle slopes as good bit of water should,

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:40.275
and it's associated with more wetlands that are that fringe the watercourse on both sides as you go up the corridor. A lot of this wetlands are actually the backyards

00:19:40.355 --> 00:19:41.875
of a lot of residences.

00:19:41.955 --> 00:19:45.875
Now I would have wished to go look in that stream too, but

00:19:47.290 --> 00:19:52.090
I've had experiences where people don't want me walking in their backyard. So I said,

00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:58.970
better not do that. Let's do a little more of a look see from afar and sort of a desktop evaluation.

00:19:59.645 --> 00:20:09.085
And then I walked the smaller stream and I think I trespassed a little bit, but only for maybe 100 feet or so before I went back into the site.

00:20:09.965 --> 00:20:12.045
What I discovered was

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:21.250
there was some flowage at that time in Eastern Stream, not a lot. As you recall, that stream

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:25.890
has a pitch to it. It has a higher grade, goes up

00:20:26.805 --> 00:20:28.165
moderately steeply,

00:20:28.325 --> 00:20:30.085
is in an incised ravine.

00:20:30.405 --> 00:20:33.525
So I walked all the way to that bathroom that I mentioned.

00:20:34.165 --> 00:20:38.245
What I discovered along the way as I turned rocks and did a cover searching

00:20:38.725 --> 00:20:42.085
was that the only life that I saw,

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:47.710
and again, I compare what I saw on that day with what I saw

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:51.870
just a few days after another stream, which is also intermittent

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:54.750
in the town of North Haven and Hampden.

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:56.510
So I have a comparison in my mind.

00:20:57.325 --> 00:20:58.765
The Hamden Stream

00:20:59.965 --> 00:21:02.525
had a slightly bigger watershed, obviously.

00:21:02.845 --> 00:21:05.485
It was loaded with macrobenthes,

00:21:06.845 --> 00:21:08.045
stoneflies and

00:21:09.085 --> 00:21:10.925
caddisflies and

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:13.320
other Macrobenthes.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:14.760
But this one only had

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:17.320
case caddisflies.

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:20.680
And this is something that I have seen over the years

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:22.760
in these streams that will

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:28.425
flow for, especially if there's a wetland upstream, they will flow for a period of time,

00:21:28.745 --> 00:21:34.745
but they dry out pretty fast or they're intermittent in the sense that they're temporarily flooded during a storm.

00:21:35.065 --> 00:21:36.425
Right after a storm,

00:21:36.985 --> 00:21:38.745
the watershed continues to

00:21:39.225 --> 00:21:40.585
put some base flow out

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:45.450
and then it dries out and then it waits for the next storm. That's exactly what these

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:48.570
case caddisflies will do,

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:50.810
is they have the ability to

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:52.890
weather the

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:59.555
differential hydrology, if you will, because they can basically shut down and keep moisture in and then wait for the next

00:22:00.275 --> 00:22:02.195
moisture event. And then they

00:22:02.515 --> 00:22:05.475
will continue on their business and foraging

00:22:05.715 --> 00:22:06.995
the substrate.

00:22:07.235 --> 00:22:09.875
So that's the only thing I saw. Did identify

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:11.520
potentially

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:13.1000
the family of

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:15.120
those species.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:16.560
Incidentally,

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:20.800
particular family is

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:23.280
indicator of very good water quality.

00:22:24.275 --> 00:22:26.675
Yeah, better Gossa somatidae.

00:22:26.915 --> 00:22:28.755
I didn't take it a genus or

00:22:29.875 --> 00:22:31.635
species. I'm sure that your

00:22:32.835 --> 00:22:36.435
chair at that time would have appreciated that, but I didn't have the time.

00:22:36.995 --> 00:22:39.235
So having had

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:45.830
that firsthand experience looking at sort of what one stream, how it differs from the other.

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:51.030
And then in my background, having some of the things that Mr. Bruner, who's here,

00:22:51.925 --> 00:22:54.085
who I've known for a very long time.

00:22:54.965 --> 00:22:56.405
Hi, Hank, how are doing?

00:22:56.805 --> 00:22:59.685
Some of the comments that he had, especially regarding

00:22:59.925 --> 00:23:02.885
the ecological integrity and connectivity of

00:23:03.525 --> 00:23:06.020
streams as the stream corridors.

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:10.020
Focused a little more on the wood turtle, which we know is

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:14.500
in very good abundance on the Hop River still, thankfully.

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:20.785
Put all that to mind. Then we had some discussions which really

00:23:20.945 --> 00:23:22.945
don't pertain as you

00:23:23.025 --> 00:23:31.025
talked earlier so much to this commission. The discussion was with the Army Corps of Engineers with a couple of staff people up there, specifically

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:35.580
Corey Rose was one of them and Paula Kohlberg was the other one,

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:38.780
to kind of get my head straight as to

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:41.260
what the core thinks about

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:44.060
even intermittent stream corridors.

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:48.095
So we had a discussion which kind of informed me a little bit going forward,

00:23:48.335 --> 00:23:51.775
something else that we will have to do pass this commission.

00:23:52.415 --> 00:23:53.455
The decision

00:23:53.695 --> 00:23:57.535
was made, I'll let Mr. Bushnell explain that a little more,

00:23:58.655 --> 00:23:58.975
that

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:02.140
the more significant corridor

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:04.540
for a number of reasons,

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:06.940
and even for wood turtle because

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:10.700
if it reaches up to this part, which is

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:13.100
questionable in my mind, but let's say it does,

00:24:13.995 --> 00:24:16.155
The topography and the

00:24:16.315 --> 00:24:17.755
width of the corridor,

00:24:18.075 --> 00:24:20.715
the quality of the corridors is much more

00:24:22.235 --> 00:24:26.650
it's it's better suited for for wildlife in general, also for wood turtle.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:32.170
Whereas the other one in the East is not because of the grade and I could not see a turtle going up

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:34.250
and spending ecological

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:38.170
capital, if you will, and energy to do that. There's nothing for it there.

00:24:38.755 --> 00:24:40.195
So the idea was,

00:24:40.515 --> 00:24:41.715
okay, let's

00:24:41.875 --> 00:24:44.435
let's put a a box culvert.

00:24:44.915 --> 00:24:45.955
Let's

00:24:46.515 --> 00:24:52.195
embed it into the substrate. Let's recreate the the watercourse underneath the intermittent watercourse.

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:54.380
Let's make sure that it passes

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:57.340
at a minimum of a fifty year storm without

00:24:57.980 --> 00:24:58.780
creating

00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:01.020
a head behind it, which was one of the

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:07.020
things that mister Hanfield had put in his report, which I see he's also in in this meeting.

00:25:07.865 --> 00:25:09.625
It also complies with

00:25:10.425 --> 00:25:14.745
Army Corps of Engineers guidelines that we will have to deal with later.

00:25:14.985 --> 00:25:16.745
So that was the the major,

00:25:17.305 --> 00:25:18.585
if you will, in

00:25:18.985 --> 00:25:22.185
my mind. Let's do this. This will be better overall,

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:25.730
allowing for this connectivity to continue without

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:27.090
restriction,

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:33.410
especially for the Western Corridor, which is in my mind the most ecologically significant.

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:36.450
In the meantime, one of the other things that I had heard from the commission,

00:25:37.345 --> 00:25:39.825
actually from my client and also from the minutes,

00:25:40.065 --> 00:25:41.745
was the question about mitigation.

00:25:42.465 --> 00:25:45.585
And that was part of my report that you see there.

00:25:48.465 --> 00:25:49.425
Typically,

00:25:49.425 --> 00:25:53.210
so we have 2,000 some square feet of disturbance,

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:59.210
at least with the previous plan, and it's probably very similar with the box culvert.

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:02.730
And typically in many, many towns, including your own,

00:26:04.595 --> 00:26:07.635
we look for opportunities for in kind mitigation,

00:26:07.795 --> 00:26:08.515
which means

00:26:08.915 --> 00:26:09.795
specifically

00:26:09.795 --> 00:26:13.155
restoration and enhancement and then creation

00:26:13.155 --> 00:26:14.115
if

00:26:14.115 --> 00:26:14.755
that

00:26:15.075 --> 00:26:17.075
opportunity exists. But usually

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:19.590
the creation and the restoration components

00:26:19.830 --> 00:26:22.470
blend together because we don't want to take

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:23.750
something

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:28.310
that's already has an ecological value, whether it's upland or wetland,

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:34.165
and take trees down to create a wetland just to create a wetland. So we always look for opportunities

00:26:34.725 --> 00:26:38.965
to restore first. If you have a degraded wetland, we go after that.

00:26:39.925 --> 00:26:41.525
If there's a wetland that's

00:26:41.860 --> 00:26:45.060
not functioning particularly well, we can enhance it, we do that.

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:49.460
And then creation is sort of the last thing that we do in that hierarchy

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:51.860
after, of course, after

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:55.380
the avoidance part. And the avoidance part is have we minimized the

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:57.935
direct impacts to the extent possible?

00:26:58.175 --> 00:27:00.815
That's the feasible prudent alternative test.

00:27:01.295 --> 00:27:02.415
Having

00:27:02.655 --> 00:27:06.415
said that, I did not see any opportunities for wetland restoration

00:27:06.415 --> 00:27:07.295
per se,

00:27:07.775 --> 00:27:08.575
or for

00:27:09.775 --> 00:27:10.975
creation on the site.

00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:15.060
But I did see some opportunities for wetland enhancement,

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:16.740
which it's

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:19.860
part of the restoration as opposed to depends on how bad it is.

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:24.500
So what I said in my report that you can see there

00:27:25.405 --> 00:27:26.365
is that

00:27:26.605 --> 00:27:30.925
as you follow that intermittent stream with a smaller watershed

00:27:31.005 --> 00:27:33.565
up the grade where the grade becomes

00:27:34.285 --> 00:27:35.725
a little more gentler,

00:27:36.125 --> 00:27:36.685
you

00:27:37.085 --> 00:27:41.840
leave this very narrow stream border, which has no wetland, no fringe wetlands.

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:45.840
And this wetland opens up to a more gentler slope.

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:52.400
And you've seen it that delineated on the site and actually extends more off the site, you can't see that. But it's

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:55.025
a significant wetland, it's possibly

00:27:55.265 --> 00:27:59.665
a little south of an acre altogether if you string everything together.

00:28:01.665 --> 00:28:03.025
That unfortunately

00:28:03.025 --> 00:28:05.105
is infected with Japanese barberry.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:09.990
And so my thought was, okay, here's an opportunity,

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:10.710
especially

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:12.870
next to the intermittent stream,

00:28:13.430 --> 00:28:19.590
to take a section of intermittent stream from the point where it flattens up coming up the corridor, which is where the

00:28:20.605 --> 00:28:22.285
where the barberries

00:28:22.285 --> 00:28:23.085
increase

00:28:23.245 --> 00:28:28.125
and follow for about a 150 linear feet, go about 15 feet on each side,

00:28:28.445 --> 00:28:29.405
and remove

00:28:29.405 --> 00:28:30.765
all of the barberry,

00:28:30.765 --> 00:28:34.125
which if you look at the understory there, and I wish I'd create a picture,

00:28:34.750 --> 00:28:39.310
I would say it's 75% of what's there. So it's an infestation.

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:40.990
And it points back to

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:43.630
the agricultural pastoral

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:48.190
use of this wetland probably several decades ago, probably in the fifties.

00:28:50.345 --> 00:28:51.065
So

00:28:51.305 --> 00:28:53.865
the idea is to remove it mechanically.

00:28:54.505 --> 00:28:55.705
No pesticides,

00:28:56.105 --> 00:28:57.305
no herbicides

00:28:57.305 --> 00:29:00.105
involved for this one species.

00:29:00.185 --> 00:29:01.545
It's very effectively

00:29:01.545 --> 00:29:03.385
removed by yanking it.

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:04.610
When

00:29:04.850 --> 00:29:08.610
the ground is moist, you can use a weed wrench or you can just

00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:12.050
snip and take it off, remove all that mass.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:14.050
So that's year one.

00:29:14.450 --> 00:29:15.730
And then year two,

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:19.010
you come back and you just remove the seedlings.

00:29:19.250 --> 00:29:20.555
In my experience,

00:29:22.795 --> 00:29:26.635
it very rarely comes back once you remove it mechanically.

00:29:26.635 --> 00:29:28.715
And so the only thing you have to deal with is

00:29:28.875 --> 00:29:34.075
a few seedlings which you yank on year two. And then the second part of the mitigation

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:36.300
plan that I was suggesting

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:37.740
is to,

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:45.980
know, in the spaces that are creating after a year, not immediately, because you want to see if anything good comes up, and you also removing the seedlings,

00:29:46.775 --> 00:29:48.215
is to look

00:29:48.535 --> 00:29:51.175
at those vacated places of the understory

00:29:51.575 --> 00:29:59.255
and put in a minimum of 50 shrubs. And the 50 shrubs would be the smaller size, so the

00:29:59.815 --> 00:30:02.055
number two pot, I guess it's a gallon.

00:30:03.590 --> 00:30:04.790
And they're usually

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:06.310
two to three feet,

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:07.430
maybe four

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:13.590
tall. The reason for that is because it is kind of rocky terrain up there. So you basically

00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:15.665
gonna have to find a small

00:30:15.985 --> 00:30:20.065
nook and cranny to put this shrub in.

00:30:20.625 --> 00:30:21.185
And

00:30:21.505 --> 00:30:27.345
so I selected a few species there that I thought based on the fact that this is a shaded area would do well.

00:30:28.340 --> 00:30:32.420
A spicebush would do well. Winterberry would do fairly well.

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:35.620
Gray dogwood, of course, always does well,

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:37.220
and nannyberry.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:44.245
The first two species are the wetter spectrum and the second two species are the slightly drier facultative.

00:30:44.245 --> 00:30:45.925
So we would have to

00:30:46.565 --> 00:30:49.205
select the appropriate areas for that.

00:30:50.005 --> 00:30:56.680
But there's a lot of microtopography already. You can choose a slightly higher area versus one that's a little lower.

00:30:56.920 --> 00:30:58.120
So that's kind of

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:00.360
in a nutshell

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:05.880
what I have come about. And of course, my conclusion is there in the end of the report

00:31:08.065 --> 00:31:10.465
tells you that I believe that

00:31:11.105 --> 00:31:13.265
this is the feasible and prudent alternative,

00:31:13.265 --> 00:31:19.825
especially something that would likely satisfy the use of Army Corps of Engineers. Although I know, I understand this is not your immediate

00:31:20.225 --> 00:31:20.705
concern,

00:31:21.070 --> 00:31:22.350
it's our concern.

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:27.950
And then of course, the mitigation there, I believe that the functions and values

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:32.990
of this wetland for the crossing and its ecological continuity

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:34.670
and integrity will continue.

00:31:35.295 --> 00:31:41.855
So that's what I have to offer and I'd be happy to answer questions. But before I do that, let me tell you that

00:31:42.175 --> 00:31:46.095
I have a double meeting in Madison that I need to get to as soon as possible. So

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:51.650
you may ask me any questions and then I'll, unfortunately, I'll have to leave.

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:56.930
>> Okay.

00:31:56.930 --> 00:31:57.650
Thank you,

00:31:57.890 --> 00:31:59.010
Mr. Logan.

00:31:59.170 --> 00:31:59.810
I will,

00:32:05.485 --> 00:32:06.285
begin

00:32:06.285 --> 00:32:11.485
coming back asking the commissioners if they have any specific questions or comments

00:32:12.765 --> 00:32:14.525
on Mr. Logan's report.

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:23.040
>> Bill Monroe, I have one question about the size of that box culvert.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:29.520
>> Yeah, I think Mr. Bushnell will be probably a better candidate for answering that question.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:31.965
>> Oh, okay. So let's

00:32:31.965 --> 00:32:35.965
hold that. I was going to ask Mr. Bushnell to comment in just a moment,

00:32:36.285 --> 00:32:42.045
but deferring to Mr. Logan's need to get to the other meeting, wanted to specifically

00:32:42.045 --> 00:32:43.005
look at

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:45.660
his comments and recommendations.

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:50.060
Was speaking for myself, was happy to see the recommendation

00:32:50.140 --> 00:32:50.700
to

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:52.940
eradicate barberry

00:32:53.500 --> 00:32:58.700
and to replace it with native shrubs, and I think these are good choices

00:32:59.075 --> 00:33:00.835
that should be successful,

00:33:01.715 --> 00:33:02.355
as

00:33:03.315 --> 00:33:07.075
Mr. Leung pointed out, in specific niches

00:33:07.395 --> 00:33:10.035
according to the kind of micro

00:33:10.275 --> 00:33:12.355
habitat preferences

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:14.730
for the individuals.

00:33:14.730 --> 00:33:15.130
But

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:16.730
I think that

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:22.490
if you look at it with a ten to fifteen year picture in mind,

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:26.730
driving across that bridge flanked by good

00:33:29.645 --> 00:33:31.165
cops of

00:33:31.885 --> 00:33:36.685
well especially spiced bush in the spring will be a real treat for the owners.

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:44.480
